{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update16.json\",\"summary\":\"Recorded the Node-wrapper proposal question on its retained receipt. CheckRun 8b3e88d4b4a641fa8ddc723ecc0ba56d (fixed experiment 7f6aa45d046c4714992282dd5e6d8389-experiment) parsed five events with no loss: the control comparison holds (parsed index 1 - while node 2 is a plain member that knows leader 1, Propose returns no error in 0ms and the leader's log index advances to 2), and both witness comparisons are violated with a complete valid witness (index 4 - after the application applied the removal and the re-add, with the leader's configuration back to voters=(1 2), node 2 caught up to the leader's log and still knowing leader 1 in the same term, Propose returns only when its 300ms deadline fires and the leader's log index stays at 4, so the proposal never reaches the group). The account is now execution-backed for the witness and its control: the source shows the channel is closed by the removal guard and restored only by a leadership change with a leader known, and the receipt shows the closed channel persists after the node is a member again with an unchanged leader. Four comparisons came back unknown because the compared fields are nested under the events' node2_state object rather than at their top level - a specification artifact whose values are visible in the same receipt's events 2 and 3, and which is not repaired by rerunning for labels; the removal and re-add premises are therefore read from those raw events while the witness's own prerequisite chain matched (correlations at indices 0-3). Counterevidence keeps the documented 'proposals can be lost' allowance and the field-nesting note, and limitations keep the one-sided wrapper harness, the 300ms deadline, the unexercised leadership-change restore path, and the open question of what the wrapper is expected to do for a re-added node.\"}"}}
